    "created_at": "2023-05-16T15:01:57Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-05-16T15:01:57Z",
    "body": "I actually wrote \"and please do not suggest getElementById()\" but I removed that because I was trying to give the benefit of the doubt in hopes of having a more open and constructive conservation with WHATWG instead of the usual chatbot-like responses.\r\n\r\nYes, I am too familiar with `getElementById` - it's in my comparison above - the point is direct access to elements with an id seems like an intuitive and desirable DOM interface, hence this inquiry about `document.all`. Just like we get with named elements in `document.forms`, direct access to elements with an id would be nice. Virtually every use of `document.getElementById('myId')` actually looks like `const myId = document.getElementById('myId')` because the API is clunky and good practice is to avoid repeating the id strings all over the place and to do the lookup just once.\r\n\r\nSo, is it feasible to fix `document.all`? \"We have no intentions of changing document.all\" is not a helpful or informative response. A little insight into why it can't or shouldn't be patched would be useful and encouraging. And if not, what about a document.forms for elements with an id? Has that ever been proposed or tried in the past? If not, any reasons why that might not be a good solution?\r\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
