    "created_at": "2023-05-16T17:31:33Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-05-16T17:35:44Z",
    "body": "> instead of the usual chatbot-like responses.\r\n\r\noof, hopefully [this](https://github.com/whatwg/dom/issues/1196#issuecomment-1544567248) didn’t come across as “chatbot-like”?\r\n\r\nYou may be interested to know, if you don’t already, that Window also exposes “named properties” (exotic getters) for elements by ID. If you find HTMLAllCollection’s API surface worthwhile for the subset of cases where it’s safe, you may prefer to leverage Window’s API surface to DX even harder :)\r\n\r\n```js\r\nwelcomeMessage.hidden = false;\r\n```\r\n\r\nThose properties suffer from the same problems, but no one’s stopping you from using them. I find it convenient for things like quick demos, though I would personally otherwise avoid them for the reasons previously described.\r\n\r\n> And if not, what about a document.forms for elements with an id? Has that ever been proposed or tried in the past? \r\n\r\nI’m not sure if I understand this question, but `document.forms` does exist and does expose named properties.\r\n\r\n> A little insight into why it can't or shouldn't be patched would be useful and encouraging.\r\n\r\nFor all of those interfaces, the current exotic getter behavior can’t be altered without breaking existing websites.",
